<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Bruce, 8th Earl of Elgin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a>–<a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lord Elgin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Bruce, 8th Earl of Elgin, was a British colonial administrator who served as governor-general of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> from 1847 to 1854. By signing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-losses-bill">Rebellion Losses Bill</a> in 1849 despite riots that burned the parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, he confirmed responsible government. He negotiated the Reciprocity Treaty of 1854 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and later served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-bruce-8th-earl-of-elgin.jpg" alt="James Bruce, 8th Earl of Elgin" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felice Beato · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Felice_Beato_(British,_born_Italy_-_Portrait_of_Lord_Elgin,_Plenipotentiary_and_Ambassador,_Who_Signed_the_Treaty_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
